Plots, in rough order of importance

One spectrum at each processing stage. A four-panel figure for a single galaxy: raw flux, continuum-normalised, log-scaled, then standardised, with the gap pixels shaded. This is the central methods figure, because it shows the reader exactly what the model receives.

Example spectra at low, typical and high SNR, for both galaxies and AGN. It shows directly that a typical galaxy spectrum (SNR ≈ 0.25) is mostly noise, which sets up your SNR discussion.

The distribution of pixel values at each stage, as histograms. This justifies the log transform: the normalised flux has extreme tails that the log version doesn't.

The rest-frame wavelength range covered at each redshift, with the main emission lines marked: Hα, Hβ, [O III] and [O II]. It shows which features fall in the observed window at each redshift, and so explains why AGN above z = 1.8 look different to the model for reasons unrelated to being AGN.

The fraction of masked pixels at each wavelength, for galaxies and AGN. It shows where the gaps are, and it will help explain why 22.8% of AGN pixels are masked against 9.5% for galaxies.

Galaxy property distributions in the train, validation and test splits, overlaid: redshift, stellar mass, sSFR, dust and H magnitude. This shows the three splits are drawn from the same population.

Galaxies against AGN: SNR, redshift and continuum level (NORM_CMN) overlaid. This is the key figure for your SNR explanation, since it shows the AGN are about 7.5 times brighter at the same noise level.

Mean against median normalisation: the two normalisers plotted against each other, and the two log-scaled flux distributions. It supports the comparison between normalisations, and shows the very small NORM_CMN values that stretch the tails of the mean-normalised flux.

Median spectra: galaxies against AGN, and galaxies in redshift bins. They show the average shape the model will learn.
